# Allocation of Funds for Agriculture and Irrigation
Tamil title: வேளாண்மைத் துறை நிதிஒதுக்கீடு (வாக்கெடுப்பு)

Speech delivered in the Legislative Assembly on 26 March 1958 during the vote on agricultural and irrigation grants.

Mr Speaker, by moving a cut in the allocation to the Irrigation Department, we on the Opposition side are pointing out the many deficiencies in the execution of irrigation schemes.

During today's debate, I thought less about the Public Works Minister, the Hon. Kakkan, than about our Finance Minister, the Hon. Subramaniam. During the previous irrigation debate, the Finance Minister spoke like a teacher instructing pupils. He said that it was natural to wish to dam and store any water seen flowing in the country, but that not enough water was actually being wasted to justify building reservoirs everywhere. He offered calculations of rainfall and water reaching the sea and argued that much of it was already used.

Today, however, honourable members from Tirunelveli, Coimbatore, Thanjavur and other districts have demanded reservoirs upon rivers in their constituencies. Their speeches compel me to conclude that the House did not accept the Finance Minister's argument. Many schemes do need to be executed. Members who refused his lesson and spoke for these works have served not merely their constituencies but the country as a whole, and I owe them thanks.

An irrigation report issued by the Congress ministry itself contains an English observation, badly damaged in this Tamil OCR, stating in substance that Madras ranked next to Punjab in the development of irrigation. Thus even under British rule some duty was recognised and performed. Yet today's ministry cannot accomplish several things requested by our own people under our own rule. That is evident from the speeches made today by ruling-party and other members about their constituencies. We must ask why our ministry cannot remedy even a portion of our deficiencies.

I had hoped that at least in discussing irrigation we might avoid the words North and South. But ruling-party members should not take satisfaction merely from comparing irrigation achievements across regions while irrigation here remains inadequate. If the answer is that wheat and rice can be grown cheaply in the North and purchased by us, the Government might as well recommend another Bhakra-Nangal or Damodar Valley project there.

When we see our State and country falling behind, the North-South issue inevitably enters your minds in another form: the relation between State and Central governments. The Minister's report repeatedly shows how dependent the State remains upon the Centre. It says, for example, that for 1958-59 the Government of India has been asked for a grant of Rs 21 lakh and a loan whose OCR figure is uncertain, and that its sanction is awaited. Elsewhere it says that a grant of Rs 6 lakh and assistance of Rs 1 lakh have been requested and sanction is awaited.

If the ministry truly believed the requested money would certainly arrive, it could state clearly that it would obtain the Central grant and loan and execute the smaller schemes members have demanded. Instead, every passage says that permission is awaited. That is why Raja Sir Muthiah Chettiar, himself of the Congress, urged the ministers to show courage and fight Delhi for the money.

Page four of the source is severely damaged, but its recoverable argument contrasts the Central Government's lavish expenditure of crores upon great dams such as Bhakra-Nangal with its neglect of smaller irrigation schemes. Even Central representatives have reportedly admitted that immense expenditure has not always produced the expected benefit. Yet our ministers say that the South lacks large rivers and therefore cannot have such dams, while also failing to secure adequate funds for its many smaller works.

You should not await permission from a distant authority holding the cash box. Look instead at our fading agriculture and the dry fields before your eyes. Waiting endlessly for sanction will yield neither benefit nor justice.

Representatives from Tirunelveli evoke that region's history in the freedom struggle and ask why such a land is being left hungry. Their voices reveal the distress of its people. The Planning Commission has stated that for the Second Five-Year Plan to succeed, agricultural production must rise by 42 per cent. If a place producing one hundred kalams produces 142 within five years, it says, we can even discharge our foreign debts. State leaders have promised that, if not 42 per cent, they will achieve a 28 per cent increase.

How can even 28 per cent be achieved while the Government says there are no great rivers for projects like Bhakra-Nangal, that the small rivers contain too little water, and that even where water exists there is no money for small irrigation schemes? If production does not rise, from what resources will foreign loans be repaid when they fall due? Though the Centre bears greater responsibility for those debts, the State cannot escape its share entirely.

Last year I pointed to a large river without water because no agreement existed between Mysore and Madras States. When such local deficiencies were discussed, the Chief Minister said members need not come here to describe each constituency's problems because he would visit the places himself. He thus inaugurated what was called the "Bagalmedu route." The ministry owes the public an account of what became of that new method.

Will it follow the Bagalmedu route again? Was it tried once and abandoned after officials advised that it was unsound? Or is it only a spectacle performed on the day after one election and the day before the next? Had the method worked, members would not now need to raise the Siriyaru, Manjalaru and other local schemes in this House.

N. Mahalingam: On a point of order, what connection has this Bagalmedu scheme with the irrigation matter now under debate?

C. N. Annadurai: Please wait. I realise the honourable Mahalingam has no connection with the Bagalmedu scheme, but that scheme has a connection with the Chief Minister. If Bagalmedu is regarded merely as an amusing national spectacle, I am entitled to ask about it.

After that experiment, I took the Chief Minister to a conference at Kundrathur to acquaint him with deficiencies in my constituency. When I later mentioned it in this House, he remarked from his seat, "You took me there and tried to deceive me." If a minister holds that view, if he will neither travel the Bagalmedu route nor accept the Kundrathur route without misconstruing it, what option remains but to raise these deficiencies here?

Does speaking here produce results? Subbiah Mudaliar himself said, "We speak and speak, but there is no result." If even he gains nothing after speaking, and if years of representations bear no fruit, what course do you leave us except to stop speaking to you and speak directly to the people? I want the rulers to understand that consequence.

You lack the courage to demand large allocations from the Centre. When we say so, some respond that they could rule even while bound to the North. As Raja Sir Muthiah Chettiar said today, when you go to Delhi you must press firmly and obtain the money. But the result depends both upon the strength of the person making the demand and the disposition of the person upon whom it is made. That is why we of the DMK object to a condition in which our State Government must continually ask permission.

The Government says negotiations with Mysore State have achieved nothing. A Zonal Council now exists, and it must examine the Palar question. Chengalpattu and North Arcot districts have no comparable alternative for river irrigation.

Either bring water into the Palar or remove its sand in great lorries and send it wherever useful. The river winding through these two districts is beautiful to see, though not a drop runs upon its surface. Irrigation experts say abundant subsoil water lies beneath it. What effort has the Government made to use that water?

Use the Zonal Council to negotiate with Mysore. If the Council is not meant for such a subject, establish an empowered goodwill delegation containing members of every party and send it to speak with the Mysore Government. Water must be brought into the Palar by every possible effort.

The people of these districts have long cherished that desire, not merely since yesterday or since voting for me. When we propose ways to bring water to the Palar, you refuse to listen or act. Yet whenever Congress ministers and their campaigners visit Kanchipuram, they ask, "Has Annadurai brought water to the Palar?" That argument achieves nothing. If water cannot be secured from Mysore, I will at least continue to press the Cheyyar reservoir project.

The Public Works Minister has known of the Cheyyar proposal for a long time. It requires no enormous expenditure and meets every qualification the Government says a scheme should possess: low cost, high benefit, few complications and no party disagreement. Congress and other-party members have jointly represented it to the Minister, who has visited the place and examined the conditions. I searched his report carefully for any mention of Cheyyar and found none.

If existing schemes are not executed and new methods are not developed, how will agriculture grow merely by saying the country has too few rivers and inadequate irrigation opportunities? Finance Minister Subramaniam offers one answer: farmers now waste too much water. If experts agree that production can be increased with much less water, explain that knowledge to the country. Send agricultural specialists to spread and demonstrate methods that conserve water and raise yields.

You cannot refuse water, demand increased food production, decline to reduce prices, and still expect the Opposition to sing your praises. Indeed, what was said today by your own party is hardly praise. It may be called ceremonial praise under party discipline, but beneath it lies powerful condemnation.

Members sometimes describe defects in a scheme and then say at the end that they support it. Pandit Nehru has himself made a pointed English observation about such qualified support, but that quotation is illegible in the OCR. I tell the Minister plainly that much of today's support is produced by party discipline, not genuine approval.

Schemes are framed without examining whether a project in one place harms another. When a member says the Sathanur project dries out Valavanur, the Minister cannot deny the concern entirely. He may say insufficient water has collected at Sathanur because rain failed. But that project slept in government offices for years precisely because it was once feared that the dam could create scarcity around Valavanur. Plans are now made without considering whether one area will be dried to benefit another.

You lack both the capacity to frame the right plans and the funds to execute those that should be framed. The report contains another English sentence damaged beyond reliable recovery. It is nevertheless true that our electrical and civil engineers have earned distinction in many parts of the country. Other places need their services because we have not devised enough worthwhile projects to employ them here. Their service elsewhere may bring us honour, but it also reveals our ministry's failure to use their abilities.

I conclude by asking the Government to consult all parties without prejudice and create the means to execute sound irrigation schemes.

---NOTES---
The printed heading labels this an agriculture allocation vote, while the speech itself repeatedly identifies the debated grant as irrigation. Both aspects are retained in the English title and introduction.

Page four is severely degraded by OCR. Its recoverable contrast between large Central dam expenditure and neglected small irrigation works is translated conservatively; unrecoverable clauses are not invented.

Several English quotations in the Tamil scan, including the irrigation-report sentence and a statement attributed to Nehru, are illegible after Tamil OCR. Their existence and surrounding argument are noted without supplying speculative wording.

The loan figure associated with the Rs 21 lakh grant appears inconsistently in OCR and must be verified directly from the scan before numerical citation.

Place-names including Bagalmedu, Siriyaru, Manjalaru, Kundrathur, Palar, Cheyyar, Sathanur and Valavanur are transliterated conservatively from the preserved source.
